LearnFromGames: the language can be given (SetOCRLanguage), and fast mode remembers the lines it read.

The original reads every line with the multi-script recognizer, asks a language ID what it read, and reads the
line again with the recognizer of the script that was guessed: on a Japanese game's screen, most lines twice and
the kanji-only ones as Chinese. A caller that knows the language now says so, and every line goes once to that
language's recognizer (Jpan for "ja"); the multi-script recognizer and the language ID are not even loaded.
 - The script recognizers have no direction head, so a line runs the way its box does: across, or down for a
   box turned a quarter.
 - The layout's confidence filter counted on that head to tell text from scenery. The stand-in value is set so
   that it drops as many lines as before: on 210 game screens cut to their text bands, 8.8 lines a screen were
   kept either way, 3.4% of the dialogue's characters were missed for 3.6%, and a read took 122 ms for 147 on
   a desktop.
With no language given, or one whose recognizer is not installed, nothing changes.

Fast mode also keeps the tensors of the 64 line images read last, by recognizer. A line whose pixels come again
(a name, a menu, the screen read a second time) is not computed again; the same pixels give the same tensors,
so the output is the same. A screen read twice in a row: 42 ms of recognition, then 2.

